WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.890
Quoting is one of the most important
ShellCheck themes because it protects argument

2
00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:09.115
boundaries.

3
00:00:09.195 --> 00:00:12.005
In many programming languages,

4
00:00:12.085 --> 00:00:18.642
a string variable remains one string when
it is passed to another function.

5
00:00:18.722 --> 00:00:25.799
In shell, an unquoted variable expansion
may be split into multiple words and may

6
00:00:25.879 --> 00:00:29.210
also be treated as a filename pattern.

7
00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:36.159
That behavior can change the number and
meaning of arguments sent to a command.

8
00:00:36.239 --> 00:00:43.213
The problem often appears with ordinary
data: paths with spaces, empty values,

9
00:00:43.293 --> 00:00:50.370
names that contain wildcard characters, or
values produced by another command.

10
00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:56.279
A script may work during testing because
the sample path is simple,

11
00:00:56.359 --> 00:01:01.042
then fail later when a real path contains
whitespace.

12
00:01:01.122 --> 00:01:08.096
It may behave unexpectedly when an empty
variable removes an argument or when an

13
00:01:08.176 --> 00:01:12.964
asterisk is interpreted as a pattern
rather than data.

14
00:01:13.044 --> 00:01:20.121
ShellCheck warnings help reviewers notice
those conditions before they become

15
00:01:20.201 --> 00:01:21.242
incidents.

16
00:01:21.322 --> 00:01:24.756
Quoting is not just a style preference.

17
00:01:24.836 --> 00:01:30.561
It is a safety control that tells the
shell to treat a value as data.

18
00:01:30.641 --> 00:01:37.094
When a variable represents one path, one
identifier, or one option value,

19
00:01:37.174 --> 00:01:40.609
quoting usually preserves the intent.

20
00:01:40.689 --> 00:01:46.621
Reviewers should ask whether the script
wants one argument or many,

21
00:01:46.701 --> 00:01:53.675
whether empty values are allowed, and
whether filename expansion is intended.

22
00:01:53.755 --> 00:02:00.312
Most of the time, preserving the value as
one argument is safer and clearer.

23
00:02:00.392 --> 00:02:07.365
There are legitimate cases where a script
intentionally relies on splitting or

24
00:02:07.445 --> 00:02:13.378
pattern expansion, but those cases should
be obvious and reviewed.

25
00:02:13.458 --> 00:02:15.748
If the script needs a list,

26
00:02:15.828 --> 00:02:21.968
consider using a safer structure
appropriate for the intended shell.

27
00:02:22.048 --> 00:02:26.316
If it needs a pattern, make that intent
explicit.

28
00:02:26.396 --> 00:02:33.577
ShellCheck is most useful when it pushes
the team to distinguish accidental shell

29
00:02:33.657 --> 00:02:37.300
interpretation from deliberate design.
